{
 "id": "french-defence",
 "name": "French Defence",
 "kind": "opening",
 "track": "openings",
 "level": "intermediate",
 "goal": "As Black against 1.e4, you can play the French Defence and know the plan against White's pawn chain: attack its base with c5 and its front with f6.",
 "priorKnowledge": [
  "pawn-structure"
 ],
 "side": "black",
 "eco": "C06",
 "puzzleTags": [
  "French_Defense"
 ],
 "puzzleThemes": [],
 "intro": {
  "text": "In the French Defence, 1...e6 and 2...d5, Black lets White build a big centre and then attacks it. White often pushes a pawn to e5, making a chain of pawns on d4 and e5 that takes space on the kingside. Black's plan is the same in almost every line: hit the base of the chain with ...c5, then its front with ...f6, until White's centre gives way and your pieces get room. The price is the light-squared bishop on c8, which stays shut in behind the e6 pawn for a while."
 },
